Top-level side effects on import; make this module a factory. Save /workspace/tasks.mjs:

import fs from "node:fs";

const DB_FILE = "/workspace/tasks.json";
if (!fs.existsSync(DB_FILE)) fs.writeFileSync(DB_FILE, "[]");
let tasks = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(DB_FILE, "utf8"));

export function addTask(title) {
  const t = { id: tasks.length + 1, title, done: false };
  tasks.push(t);
  fs.writeFileSync(DB_FILE, JSON.stringify(tasks));
  return t;
}
export function completeTask(id) {
  const t = tasks.find((x) => x.id === id);
  if (!t) throw new Error("no such task");
  t.done = true;
  fs.writeFileSync(DB_FILE, JSON.stringify(tasks));
  return t;
}
export function listOpen() { return tasks.filter((t) => !t.done); }

Merely importing it writes to disk, and all tests share one file. Refactor to export createTaskStore(storage) where storage is { load(): array, save(array): void }; provide fileStorage(path) and memoryStorage() implementations. No module-level I/O whatsoever. Write tasks.test.mjs (node:test): with memoryStorage, add/complete/listOpen flows, error on unknown id, ids stay unique after completing then adding (expose the id-reuse bug in the original id scheme: after removing nothing it is fine, but make ids monotonic via a counter persisted in storage and test that adding 2, completing 1, adding 1 more yields ids 1,2,3); with fileStorage pointed at a temp dir (node:os tmpdir + mkdtemp), state survives across two createTaskStore calls. node --test green and importing the module must create no files (test that too).

